Found and fixed four defects behind both symptoms. Nothing was committed, and the two worker fixes still need a deploy to reach users.

## 1. Sign-in bounces you back out (session revoked by the client itself)

[client.ts](apps/web/src/lib/cloud/client.ts#L256)

Access tokens expire after 30 minutes, and the access token lives in `sessionStorage` — so after every app restart the first cloud calls need a refresh. The Worker's refresh tokens are **single-use and rotate**, and a second use is treated as theft: it revokes the whole session chain and answers `refresh_reused`. The client refreshed once *per request*, so the account screen (which loads sessions + devices together, plus the backup list and Telegram status) fired several refreshes with the same token. One rotated it; the next was read as reuse; the client received `refresh_reused` and called `clearSession()` — the exact "Sign in again to continue" the users report, usually seconds after a successful sign-in.

Fixed with a shared single-flight refresh (one rotation for every caller) plus a guard so an in-flight attempt cannot overwrite or clear a session that was replaced by a newer sign-in. [cloudRefreshRace.test.ts](apps/web/test/cloudRefreshRace.test.ts) models the Worker's rotation/reuse semantics; with the guard disabled it fails with `CloudError: Sign in again to continue`, and it passes with the fix.

## 2. Telegram's deep link was never opened in the packaged app

[TelegramIntegrationCard.tsx](apps/web/src/features/account/TelegramIntegrationCard.tsx) used `window.open(res.deepLink, '_blank')` *after* awaiting the link request. In the Tauri webview that is dropped by the popup blocker (the link is only known after the await), and an in-app window has no protocol handler for `t.me` — so the bot handoff never happened. It now goes through the existing https-only `open_external` IPC via a small helper ([openExternal.ts](apps/web/src/lib/openExternal.ts)), the same path DnsPage uses, and the pending block also shows an "open the bot" button so a browser-mode block is recoverable. Covered by [openExternal.test.ts](apps/web/test/openExternal.test.ts).

## 3. "Refresh Subscriptions" blanked every subscription card

[cloud/worker/src/index.ts](cloud/worker/src/index.ts#L1026)

`GET /subscriptions` returns the documented safe model (`id`, `plan`, `expiration_time`, `total_gb`, …), but `POST /integrations/telegram/sync` returned the puller's internal `NetpulseEntitlement[]` (`subscriptionId`, `expiresAt`, `totalGb`, …). The card renders one type, so after clicking Refresh every plan read "Unnamed plan", every status "Unknown", and traffic "0.00 GB used". The sync route now returns the same projection as the list route; the worker test asserts the keys so the two cannot drift again.

## 4. Unlinking in Telegram permanently blocked re-linking

[netpulse-integration.ts](cloud/worker/src/netpulse-integration.ts#L486)

`link.revoked` was accepted and audited but never applied to `np_links`. The app kept showing "Telegram linked" after the user unlinked in Telegram, and because the local row stayed `active`, a fresh link attempt was refused with `409 already_linked` — a dead end. The signed event now marks the link unlinked and clears the entitlement mirror, so re-linking works. Tested in [netpulse-integration.test.ts](cloud/worker/test/netpulse-integration.test.ts).

**Verification:** worker 93/93, web 361/361, `tsc --noEmit` clean in both, production web build succeeds. I also booted the sidecar and exercised the real `POST /cloud/egress` path against the live worker: health 200, login answers the expected 401 for bad credentials, Telegram routes 401 without a session — so the server-side sign-in path itself is healthy; a remaining sign-in failure with no stored session is network reachability, which the UI already attributes to the proxy.

**Not done:** worker deploy (production publishing needs your go-ahead), and an end-to-end Telegram link still needs a real Telegram user or a staging bot. One caveat: eslint flags two unused symbols in `cloud/worker/src/index.ts` from the uncommitted provisioning feature (`ProvisionResponseSchema`, `generateVpnConfig`'s `subscriptionId`); they predate this change and I left them alone.

The worker fixes only take effect once deployed — the refresh-race and deep-link fixes ship with the next desktop build.
