**Result: the three reported exits — middle-click, drag-and-drop links, and raw anchors — are closed by one document-level guard, tested, and verified in a real Chromium engine. One class of exit remains, and it needs a Rust change rather than a web one.**

**What the audit found.** Only `ExternalUrlLink` routed external clicks to the OS opener, and only for a plain left click. Everything else was unguarded:

- `auxclick` (middle-click) was not handled anywhere, so WebView2 opened an in-app window with no browser chrome — the same symptom the opener exists to prevent.
- No `drop`/`dragover` handler existed in the entire web app, so dragging a link onto the page navigated the webview.
- Any anchor not rendered through the component — raw markup, data-driven links, future code — navigated the app away from itself.
- Ctrl/Cmd/Shift+click and Enter on a focused anchor are ordinary `click`s on anchors the component never saw.
- No `window.open` call sites outside the opener's web fallback, no external `<iframe>`/`<object>`/`<form action>`, and the internal `/ip`-style anchors are in-app only (Tauri's asset resolver falls back to `index.html`; checked in the `tauri-2.11.5` source).

**What changed.** [externalNavigation.ts](apps/web/src/lib/externalNavigation.ts) installs four capture-phase listeners on the document — `click`, `auxclick`, `dragover`, `drop` — installed in [main.tsx](apps/web/src/main.tsx) before the first render. An absolute `http(s)` URL on another origin goes to `openExternalUrl`; same-origin routes, fragments and non-web schemes are left to the app. Link and file drags are cancelled so the engine cannot navigate; a URL dropped as `text/plain` is caught on `drop`; plain-text `dragover` is deliberately left alone so dragging text into an input still works. [ExternalUrlLink.tsx](apps/web/src/components/ui/ExternalUrlLink.tsx) now skips its own handler when the event is already `defaultPrevented` (otherwise the guard and the component would open two tabs) and handles middle-click itself for contexts without the guard.

