The operator path is in place: a token-gated rotation endpoint plus a panel on the existing `/ops` page.

**`POST /api/v1/ops/vpn/rotate`** — [ops-vpn.ts](cloud/worker/src/ops-vpn.ts), wired in [index.ts](cloud/worker/src/index.ts#L280-L286):

```jsonc
{ "email": "customer@example.com" }                        // every protocol
{ "userId": "usr_…" }                                      // every protocol
{ "email": "customer@example.com", "protocol": "xray-vmess-ws" }  // one
```

- Guarded by the shared [Ops gate](cloud/worker/src/ops-auth.ts): fail-closed `503` when `OPS_TOKEN` is unset, constant-time compare, and its **own** 10-per-5-minutes bucket so a burst of status reloads can't throttle an incident rotation (or vice versa).
- Body validation is strict: exactly one identifier (both would make "which config dies" ambiguous), and only the five per-user protocols — a shared-secret protocol answers `400 unsupported_protocol` because there is nothing per-account to rotate.
- Lookup is by `normalized_email`, so a mixed-case legacy row is still found; disabled accounts are refused (`409 account_disabled`) rather than minted a new identity.

**What it actually does** — [rotateVpnCredentials](cloud/worker/src/vpn-users.ts#L86):

1. Replaces every matching `vpn_credentials.secret` in **one D1 batch**, so a partial rotation can't leave one protocol on the leaked value. `status` is deliberately untouched: rotating a revoked identity changes its secret but never re-grants access.
2. Rebuilds the stored `vpn_profiles` from the new secret and bumps `config_version`, so the Account page's config stops showing the leaked identity. Only provisioning's `vpn_vp_*` rows are touched — the desktop's `np-local-…` envelopes stay empty.
3. Writes `vpn.credentials.rotated` to `audit_events` (target account in `user_id`, actor as `by:ops-token`).
4. Returns `previousFingerprint → fingerprint` per protocol, `profilesUpdated`, and `syncWindowSeconds: 60`. The new secret **never** appears in the response — the customer gets it through the normal provision/sync path.

The leaked config dies when the VPN host's next reconcile (≤ 60 s, the existing systemd timer) applies the new set. That is the honest deadline for "certainly dead", and it's why the number ships in the response.

**Operator UI** — [ops-page.ts](cloud/worker/src/ops-page.ts#L450-L465) gets a "Rotate VPN credentials" card (email or `usr_` id, optional protocol, confirmation, result line). It writes everything with `textContent`, so the response can't inject markup, and the rotate script is separate from the snapshot renderer the page deliberately keeps server-side.

**Docs** — [README.md](cloud/worker/README.md#L158) has the curl recipes, the ordered effects, and the one limitation worth telling customers: an already-imported third-party client needs a manual re-import, because the old identity is dead regardless.

**Verified**
- [ops-vpn.test.ts](cloud/worker/test/ops-vpn.test.ts): 15 tests through the real fetch handler against miniflare D1 — gate (401/503/405, ambiguity, unsupported protocol, unknown account, disabled, no credentials, tighter rate limit with the status page still working), and effect: every secret replaced, the response and the `vpn_profiles` rebuild contain no secret, the host pull's `version` changes and none of the old secrets survive, single-protocol rotation leaves the rest byte-identical, revoked stays revoked and absent from the pull, normalized-email lookup, `np-local-*` envelopes untouched.
- Full worker suite `125 passed`, `tsc --noEmit` clean, and the `/ops` inline script parse-checked by extracting it in Node (it isn't covered by the snapshot tests by design).
- Not verified: a live deployment and the real host reconcile — the ≤ 60 s window is the existing reconciler contract, not something this change measured.
