 * Card entrances — the page's movement, handed one level down.
 *
 * `page-enter` moves the routed page as ONE surface. On a page made of cards
 * that leaves every card indistinguishable from the page, so the content reads
 * as arriving all at once. A `stagger` container lets each direct child settle
 * 8px after the one before it: the page still leads, and the cards then resolve
 * in the order they are read (top-left first) instead of as a slab.
 *
 * The index is a custom property set by `:nth-child`, not inline style and not
 * JS: a card that mounts later (a query resolving, a fold opening) joins the
 * same rhythm with no hook, and a re-render never replays it because the
 * animation runs only when the element is created.
 *
 * Capped at 8. Past that the last card would arrive after the user has already
 * started reading the first, which reads as lag, not as choreography — items 9+
 * share the final slot instead of queueing further.
 *
 * Fill is `backwards`, never `both`, for the same reason `page-enter` is: a
 * retained transform (even `none`, which the browser reports as an identity
 * matrix) makes the element a containing block for `position: fixed`
 * descendants, and these cards can host panels that must be sized to the window.
 */
