Done — the WebDriver test now proves the handoff, and running it for real surfaced (and fixed) a defect in the test itself.

**What the test asserts now** ([navigation_webview.rs](src-tauri/tests/navigation_webview.rs))

| Step | Assertion |
|---|---|
| `location.href = 'https://example.com/'` | refused (URL and document unchanged) **and** the app logged `handing example.com to the OS browser` **and** the launch path logged `opening external link for host example.com` **and** the handoff count rose by exactly one **and** `could not open the URL` is absent |
| `window.open('https://example.com/','_blank')` | no second webview **and** `new-window backstop: opening example.com externally instead` **and** handoff count +1 |
| `location.href = 'http://example.com/'` | refused **and** the handoff count did **not** move — a refused URL launches nothing |
| `location.hash = '#/vpn'` | in-app routing still works (a policy, not a blanket block) |

Refusing silently and refusing-with-handoff were previously indistinguishable, so a `classify` that dropped every navigation would have passed. The OS browser is a process the test cannot drive, so the handoff is observed through the two lines the app writes around the spawn; I noted in [os_open.rs](src-tauri/src/os_open.rs#L229) and [window.rs](src-tauri/src/window.rs#L52) that those phrases are now asserted, so nobody rewords them by accident.

**A real defect the extension exposed.** The first real run failed not in my new checks but in the pre-existing "document is still the app" one: `chrome-error://chromewebdata/`. The debug binary resolves its window to `build.devUrl` (Tauri sets its `dev` cfg for a debug build), and WebDriver's `GET /url` answers with the *requested* URL — which stays `http://localhost:5173/` on an error page. So the test's readiness gate had been passing against a window that never loaded anything. The wait now requires `document.location.href` to be the app document too. Since `.github/workflows/ci.yml` built with plain `cargo build` and never served that origin, the `webview-navigation` job would now fail for an unrelated reason, so it starts Vite, waits for HTTP 200 (failing with the server's log if it never answers), and stops it after the test.

**Evidence.** `cargo build` clean, `cargo check --tests` clean, and with an msedgedriver matching WebView2 155.0.4283.62:

```
test the_navigation_backstop_holds_in_a_real_webview ... ok
test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; finished in 6.50s
cargo test --bins navigation → 12 passed; 0 failed
```

The app's own durable log from that run, which is the handoff proof:

```
[info] navigation backstop: handing example.com to the OS browser
[info] opening external link for host example.com
[info] main window loaded http://localhost:5173/
[info] new-window backstop: opening example.com externally instead
[info] opening external link for host example.com
[warn] navigation backstop: refused http://example.com (the app never loads it and never opens it)
```

Written up in [NAVIGATION_HANDOFF_WEBVIEW_PROOF_2026-10-26.md](reports/ui-upgrade/NAVIGATION_HANDOFF_WEBVIEW_PROOF_2026-10-26.md), with the journal's "was not run" entry replaced by the runtime result.

**Limits, stated plainly.** No assertion can see the user's actual browser window: handoff success is the launch path not reporting an error (a fitter claim would need a launcher seam). This is Windows/WebView2 only — WebKitGTK is untested — and the CI job itself has not run on GitHub from here. The matching `msedgedriver` is a local download in `tmp/msedgedriver/` (untracked), and I stopped the Vite server I started; nothing was committed or deployed, so the change sits with the rest of the uncommitted mission work.

The handoff proof currently rides on log wording, which is a coupling worth removing if you want this to be a long-lived guarantee.
