Done. Here's what I built and what I could prove.

## The defect that made the request impossible as stated

A node's fingerprint deliberately excludes the credential, so a re-issued credential produces the *same* node — `importParsedConfigs` classified it as a duplicate, and the duplicate branch wrote the vault **only when it was empty**. Once a credential rotated, the retired secret stayed in the vault forever: every probe failed authentication, honestly and permanently, and no import could ever repair the node because it was a duplicate by construction. The only re-issue path was the Account page, which most subscribers never open.

## The mechanism ([nodesService.ts](apps/api/src/services/nodesService.ts))

A manual/provider-supplied import may now **replace** a differing stored credential and reports how many it replaced (`credentialsUpdated`, also recorded on the `node.duplicate` / `node.manual-import` events). Only that path may: a subscription feed carrying the same host with a *shared* credential must never overwrite a per-user one, so sources keep the backfill-only rule. An empty incoming secret never clears anything, and a vault write failure is reported as "not replaced" rather than as a successful refresh.

## The surfaces ([provisionedRepair.ts](apps/web/src/features/vpn/provisionedRepair.ts), [ProvisionedRepairCard.tsx](apps/web/src/features/vpn/ProvisionedRepairCard.tsx), mounted in [VpnPage.tsx](apps/web/src/features/vpn/VpnPage.tsx))

The card is mounted once in the VPN shell, so every engine surface sees it, and it renders nothing unless there is something to act on. It detects provisioned profiles (`NetPulse · `) that have never passed, or stopped passing, a probe — and says that, not "your credential expired", because a failing probe has causes the app cannot tell apart locally. One click runs: `provision()` → `toShareLink` per profile → `POST /nodes/import` (the replacing path) → a `proxy` probe of the affected nodes. The verdict is evidence-based: `credentialsUpdated > 0` proves the stored credential was stale; `0` says the server issued the identical credential and the cause is elsewhere; no probe means the message does not claim the node works.

## Evidence actually run

- **End-to-end over the real stack** ([provisionedCredentialRotation.test.ts](apps/api/test/provisionedCredentialRotation.test.ts), 4 passed): real HTTP route, real encrypted vault file, real share links. Import A → vault holds A; import B (same endpoint, new credential) → `duplicates: 1, credentialsUpdated: 1` and the vault holds **B**; re-import B → `0`; re-import A (a stale saved link) → `1`. It asserts the vault's *contents*, because "the server stopped publishing the old credential" is a weaker claim than "the app no longer holds it". The same test also proves the card's own query (`GET /nodes?search=NetPulse · `) finds the profile with its marker.
- **Service-level rule** ([importCredentialRefresh.test.ts](apps/api/test/importCredentialRefresh.test.ts), 7 passed), including that a feed cannot clobber a per-user credential.
- **Web logic** ([provisionedRepair.test.ts](apps/web/test/provisionedRepair.test.ts), 18 passed): detection cases, and that a same-credential refresh never reads like a repair.
- **Regression**: `npm test -w @netpulse/api` → 98 files / 1064 tests pass; `npm test -w @netpulse/web` → 48/49 files, 431 tests pass; `tsc --noEmit` clean in both apps; eslint clean on the changed source files.

## Limitations and one unrelated repair

I could not exercise the refresh against the **live Worker** `POST /api/v1/provision` (no Cloud session/entitlement here), so the control-plane half is unproven. The installed app's database has 52,573 nodes and **0** with the provisioned marker, so the card has never rendered against a real subscription profile on this machine — detection was proven on an app instance built in a temporary data directory. The Worker's own rotation policy (when it re-issues) is not tested.

Two things belong to someone else and I flagged rather than absorbed them: [provisionProfiles.test.ts](apps/web/test/provisionProfiles.test.ts) (13 tests) targets the pre-refactor sing-box import API and was failing identically *before* I touched anything, and its in-flight refactor had left [AccountPage.tsx](apps/web/src/features/account/AccountPage.tsx) calling a renamed export, which broke the web typecheck. I renamed that one call site so the app builds again; the stale test file is still its author's. Full record: [02_PROVISIONED_CREDENTIAL_REPAIR.md](reports/engines/02_PROVISIONED_CREDENTIAL_REPAIR.md).

The natural next step is to close the gap I could not: prove the refresh against the real control plane, so a rotating credential is repaired by the deployed Worker and not just by the app's own API.
